			Chapter 11 -- The AOS/VS Patch Utility


                The AOS/VS Patch  Utility  (PATCH)  allows  you  to  check,
                enter, and install patches in all AOS/VS data files.  PATCH
                is generally used to modify  both  16-  and  32-bit  AOS/VS
                program files, but in a manner which is somewhat  different
                from the File Editor Utility (FED).  PATCH allows a develo-
                per to create a file -- the patchfile -- which  contains  a
                list of patch lines.  Each of these patch lines will affect
                a single location, one 16-bit word, in the  file  which  is
                being patched -- the  target  file.   Certain  features  of
                PATCH give flexibility to the  developer  which  cannot  be
                achieved with FED.  Among these  features  are  conditional
                and relocatable patching which will be discussed later.


                             PATCH Operating Procedures

                CLI Command Line

                The CLI command format to execute PATCH is:

	|	XEQ PATCH/T=targetfile/P=patchfile/function [/filespec]

                where:

                targetfile    is the file to be patched. This switch is
                              required.

                patchfile     is the file that contains the patch lines.
                              This switch is required.

                function      is a switch that  tells  PATCH  what you
                              want to do.  Either /C, /E, /I, or /Y is
                              required.  More than one function switch
                              is not allowed.

                filespec      is a switch  that  defines either the
                              symbol table file or the patch history file.
                              This switch is optional. /S and /L can both
                              present in a single command line.

                                 Switches to PATCH

                All specifications to PATCH are done through switches. (See
	|	Table 1, below, for a complete list of the PATCH switches.)
                Each PATCH switch has a descriptive full name that you  can
                abbreviate to a single letter.  The order of switches in  a
                command line does not matter.

