                               Filenaming Conventions

                PATCH generates the overlay and symbol table  filenames  by
                stripping any .PR or .SY extensions from  the  target  file
                and appending .OL and .ST, respectively. You can explicitly
                specify the symbol table filename by using the /S= switch.

                PATCH names the patch history file by appending .PH to  the
                filename of the target  file,  including  any  .PR  or  .SY
                extension.

                In the following examples, the commands on the  left  cause
                PATCH to use the filenames indicated below each example:

                Example 1:
                    X PATCH/P=PATCHFILE/I/T=TARGET
                    X PATCH/P=PATCHFILE/I/T=TARGET.PR

                Symbol table filename = TARGET.ST
                Overlay filename = TARGET.OL
        
                Patch  history filename = TARGET.PH,
                and TARGET.PR.PH respectively.

                Example 2:
                    X PATCH/P=PATCHFILE/I/T=TARGET/S=MYSYM

                Symbol table filename = MYSYM
                Target file = TARGET


                   Important Information about Installing Patches

	|	AOS/VS provides automatic patching of system files via the
	|	Update tool.  You should use the Update tool to  patch the
	|	system files, but if you are curious about which files are
	|	patched, the pathnames of the files (and associated symbol
	|	table files, see the document :UPDATE:UPDATE.DOC, the text
	|	of the patch files themselves, and the update script files
	|	that are part of the update themselves.


                                Writing Patch Files

                A developer can create a patch file  by  using  the  /ENTER
                switch or by  using  a  text  editor.   The  /ENTER  switch
                provides an interactive check of patch lines  as  they  are
                entered.  For example, if you use an illegal expression  in
                a line, PATCH will inform you after you finish  typing  the
                line.  A patch file can contain up to 2000 patch lines.


									11-4

	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                Examples of patch files can be found in the appendices.

                Patch Line Format

                        PATCH is a line processor.  A line  is  up  to  255
                        characters  terminated  by  either  a  newline,   a
                        carriage return, or a form feed.   Non-empty  lines
                        which end with an EOF will cause an error.
        
                        There are 2 command line formats.

                        1.  Comment Format

                        2.  Command Format


                        Comment Format

                        PATCH allows you to make comments that appear  only
                        in the @OUTPUT file and  the  patch  history  file.
                        These comments must be in the following format:

                                ;yourcomment

                        The first non-blank character  of  a  comment  line
                        must be a semicolon.


                        Command Format

                        Each patch line can be up to  255  characters  ter-
                        minated by NEW LINE, CR (carriage return), or  FORM
                        FEED.   PATCH  reports  any  non-empty  line   that
                        terminates in EOF as an error.

                        There are two types of patch lines: Non-update  and
                        update commands.


                        Non-update commands

                        A non-update command has a percent sign (%) as  its
                        first character.  Non-update commands do not modify
                        any  file.   Instead,  they  set   up   the   patch
                        environment.  The non-update commands are:

                        * %PROGRAM - This command indicates that  you  want
                        the target file to be in program format.  (This  is
                        the default command.)



									11-5

	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                        * %OVERLAY - This command indicates that  you  want
                        to patch the overlay file that is  associated  with
                        the target file.  (Use  this  command  with  16-ibt
                        program files only.)

                        * %USERFILE -  This  command  patches  that  target
                        file, which is a user data file.

                        * %AOSVS - This command patches that  target  file,
                        which is an AOS/VS system file.

                        When you issue  a  non-update  command,  subsequent
                        update commands will be applied  in  the  mode  you
                        have  specified.   If  you  specify  %OVERLAY,  the
                        overlay  file  for  the  current  program  will  be
                        modified.  You can issue a new command at any point
                        in a patch.  If  you  do  not  issue  a  non-update
                        command,  PATCH  assumes  the  default   non-update
                        command, %PROGRAM.  You can  abbreviate  non-update
                        commands, as long as  the  abbrevia-  tions  remain
                        unique.


			Update Commands

			Update commands  modify  16-bit words.   An  update
			command  defines  the  32-bit logical address  of a
			word,  the  word's  current contents, the  proposed
			contents,  and an optional condition that, if false
			(zero), prevents the patch line from being applied.
			If there is no condition, the default is true. (Any
			non-zero conditional is true.)

                        The format for update commands is:

                                location/old/new/<condition>/

                        where:

                        location (required)
                        is where you want the patch to occur.  Location can
                        be any legal  PATCH  expression  (except  null  and
                        period(.)) that maps to an address within the file.
                        (You cannot append to the target file.)

                        old (required)
                        is the current (pre-patch)  contents  of  location.
                        Old can be any legal PATCH expression.




									11-6

	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                        new (required)
                        is the proposed contents of location.  New  can  be
                        any legal PATCH expression.

                        condition (optional)
                        allows you to decide under what  circumstances  you
                        want to apply  the  patch.   You  do  not  need  to
                        specify a condition if you always want to apply the
                        patch.  If PATCH evaluates the  condition  and  the
                        result is  nonzero  (true),  then  it  applies  the
                        patch; if the result is zero (false), then it  does
                        not apply the patch.  Condition can  be  any  legal
                        PATCH  expression.   You  do  not  have  to  define
                        symbols.  PATCH treats any undefined  symbol  as  a
                        symbol equal to zero (false).  This allows  you  to
                        write patches that  PATCH  only  applies  when  the
                        symbol exists.

                        / (A separator is required)
                        can be any one of  the  following  separators  that
                        delimits the end of a field and instructs PATCH how
                        to evaluate that field:

                        / (Slash)
                        Expression has a 16-bit result.  (Do not use  slash
                        with the null expression.)

                        \ (backslash)
                        Expression has a doubleword result.   (do  not  use
                        backslash with the null expression.)

                        tab
                        Expression has a 16-bit result.

                        space
                        Expression has a 16-bit result.

                        No arguments to either type of commands are legal.














