	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                PATCH Expressions

                        Every field can  contain  an  expression  --  PATCH
                        accepts all of  the  debugger  expressions.   Also,
                        PATCH accepts all of the debugger operators  except
                        for:

                                o Close bracket (])

                                o Period (.) in the location field

                        You can use hatch (^) in place of period (.) in the
                        location field.  (See "Symbolic Expressions"  below
                        for more information on hatch.)

                        Assembly language instructions  and  floating-point
                        constants  are  special  expressions.    You   must
                        enclose them in brackets, "[]" or "{}" in order  to
                        prevent PATCH from confusing  their  contents  with
                        separators  and   operators   (see   "Instructions"
                        below).


                        Null Expressions

                        The null expression is an empty field  (except  for
                        tabs and spaces) enclosed in brackets ([]).

                        When you specify an old or new field that  is  more
                        than one  word  long,  PATCH  uses  only  the  most
                        significant word.  Then, when you  issue  the  next
                        update  command,  PATCH  expects  to  find  a  null
                        expression in the same field.  PATCH then  replaces
                        the null expression with the next most  significant
                        word.  If PATCH finds too many or not  enough  null
                        expressions in subsequent patch lines,  it  reports
                        an error.

                        It is an error to use  a  null  expression  in  the
                        location field.  Null expressions in the  condition
                        field default to true.











									11-8

	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                        Symbolic Expressions

                        PATCH  supports  all  debugger  symbols,  including
                        integers, decimal integers,  and  program  symbols.
                        However, PATCH treats  assembly  language  instruc-
                        tions and floating-point numbers as special expres-
                        sions (see "Instructions" below).

                        A hatch (^) symbol in the patch line  causes  PATCH
                        to replace the hatch with the string from the  same
                        field in the previous update command.  For example:

                        location           old           new            con
                        LABEL+2            10            5
                        ^+1                ^+6           ^+2

                        In this case, the second patch line  is  equivalent
                        to:

                        LABEL+2+1          10+6          5+2

                        The values in the  old  and  new  fields  from  the
                        previous  update  command  must  have   single-word
                        values.   Patch  lines  with  double-word   results
                        require that a null expression  follow  the  second
                        word.

                        Note that if you  use  a  symbol  in  the  previous
                        update command, it will be  re-evaluated  when  the
                        hatch is expanded.  For  example,  the  symbol  "."
                        used in  the  old  field  of  the  previous  update
                        command will have a new value  when  the  hatch  is
                        expanded on the current line.


                        Indirect Expressions

                        To perform indirection, use one  of  the  following
                        unary operators:

                        @ resolves a single-word value at the location that
                        you indicate.

                        # resolves double-word value at the  location  that
                        you indicate.

                        Both  operators  have  precedence  over  all  other
                        operators, except parenthesis.

									11-9

	AOS/VS Debugger and File Editor User's Manual		4-Feb-93
	Documentation Changes, Revision 4



                        For example,

                        @X+1 finds the 16-bit contents of X in  the  target
                        file and adds 1.

                        @(x+1) finds the 16-bit contents of location x+1.

                        #Y*2 finds the 32-bit contents of Y and  multiplies
                        by 2.

                        @@X finds the 16-bit contents of  the  16-bit  con-
                        tents of X.

                        When performing indirection,  PATCH  will  use  the
                        most recent value of a target location.  Therefore,
                        if you have modified "X" previously in  your  patch
                        file, then PATCH will use the modified  value  when
                        it resolves indirection.

                        Note that if you use  the  "#"  sign  in  a  normal
                        instruction  (i.e.,  "[XJMP  #LOCTN]")  PATCH  will
                        abort with an error "CHANNEL NOT  OPEN."   Using  a
                        "#" in an instruction is only allowed  in  instruc-
                        tions   surrounded   by   curly   brackets.     See
                        "Instructions" below.


                Instructions

                Assembly language instructions  can  be  specified  in  two
                ways.  If an instruction is surrounded with square brackets
                (i.e., "[XJMP PSPACE+23]"), then it is  assembled  verbatim
                -- no special actions are taken by PATCH  to  evaluate  the
                instruction before it is assembled.

                If an instruction is surrounded with curly brackets  (i.e.,
                "{LJMP #PSPACE}"), then special action will be taken if  an
                "@" or a "#" is found in the instruction.  Within the curly
                brackets, any occurrence of these two symbols indicates  to
                PATCH that an indirect expression is being flagged.   PATCH
                will process  the  expression  before  the  instruction  is
                assembled (see "Indirect Expressions" above).

                Note that indirect memory reference instructions (i.e. "LDA
                1,@ADDR") and no-load instructions (i.e.,  "SUB#  1,2,SNR")
                cannot be assembled in the  curly  bracket  syntax  because
                PATCH gives special meaning to "@" and "#".




