	Documentation Changes, Revision 4


                Writing Relocatable Patches

                Often a developer will want to place several patches into a
                single patch file.  When this is done, the  developer  must
                keep track of where his patch is being installed  into  the
                program file.  If a developer needs to put the  patch  into
                reserved patch space (PSPACE), then it is  likely  that  he
                will need to put a  branch  instruction  somewhere  in  the
                normal code in order to activate the patch.  The  developer
                should use the curly bracket instruction syntax  to  accom-
                plish this branch.

                The curly bracket instruction syntax can  be  used  if  the
                first two words of PSPACE are reserved for  use  by  PATCH.
                These two words should always  contain  a  pointer  to  the
                first free word of patch space.  Initially, the  first  two
                words of PSPACE should contain the address PSPACE+2.  After
                each patch is installed into PSPACE, the address in  PSPACE
                should  be  updated  to  point  to  the  first  free  patch
                location.

                By maintaining PSPACE in this fashion, the  developer  need
                not concern himself with the actual location of a patch  in
                PSPACE; when a patch's location  needs  to  be  known,  the
                developer can reference the address located  in  the  first
                double-word of PSPACE.  For example, the instruction  {LJMP
                #PSPACE} could be used as a branch  instruction  to  invoke
                the patch.  When PATCH assembles the instruction,  it  will
                look at the first double-word of  PSPACE,  and  PATCH  will
                treat it as the target address  of  the  LJMP  instruction.
                Note that the resulting instruction will be a branch to the
                first location of the current patch's reserved patch space.

                In order to specify the location fields for the patch lines
                which will go in PSPACE, the developer  can  use  "#PSPACE"
                for the first line and "^+1" for  successive  lines.   This
                way, the patch will  be  installed  sequentially,  starting
                from the first free location.

                Patches which are written this way are position-independent
                because they do not have any  special  offsets  coded  into
                their instructions.  Several  advantages  arise  from  this
                method.  First, a patch can  be  modified  by  a  developer
                without affecting other patches in the patch file.  Even if
                a patch grows or shrinks, other patches will  be  relocated
                accordingly.  Second, a patch can be  moved  around  within
                the patch file without any problem.  And finally, a  condi-
                tional patch will not waste patch space  in  a  program  to
                which it does not apply, because  the  developer  will  not
                need to leave a hole to accomodate it.  Appendix B contains
                an example patch file with relocatable patches.
        

